<Gallery title="Calendar">
  <p class="demo-note">
    A month grid that says <strong>which days are special</strong> — public holidays, a
    plant shutdown, period-end, the dates a shipment cannot land on. Use it to
    <em>show</em> a calendar, and — with each day as a real submit button — to
    <strong>pick from one</strong> when which days are special is part of the choice.
    For a plain date with no such context, use the native
    <code>&lt;input type="date"&gt;</code>: it is localized, one tab stop, and native on
    a phone.
  </p>

  <section class="demo">
    <h2>One month, with the days that matter marked</h2>
    <Demo code={single} />
    <p class="bo-u-text-muted">Weekends are hatched, the holiday carries a dot, today is
    outlined and the chosen day is filled. Every one of those is a shape as well as a
    colour, and each writes its meaning into the cell as visually-hidden text — so the
    grid reads the same in greyscale, under forced colours, and to a screen reader.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="demo">
    <h2>Where the week starts is your data, not a setting here</h2>
    <div class="demo-pair">
      <div class="demo-pair__preview" set:html={weekMonday} />
      <div class="demo-pair__preview" set:html={weekSunday} />
    </div>
    <p class="bo-u-text-muted">Monday-first is ISO&nbsp;8601 and usual across
    Europe and much of Asia; <strong>Sunday-first is standard in the US, Canada,
    Japan and much of Latin America</strong>. An ERP shipped to a US buyer with a
    Monday-first delivery calendar is simply wrong for them, so both must be
    possible.</p>
    <p class="bo-u-text-muted"><strong>The framework deliberately ships no
    <code>data-week-start</code>.</strong> There is nothing for it to do: this
    CSS contains no positional rule — no <code>nth-child</code>, and
    &ldquo;closed&rdquo; is a per-cell attribute you set, not a column. Week
    start changes which date sits in which cell, which is decided where you
    generate the month. An attribute that styled nothing would be API we could
    never remove.</p>
    <p class="bo-u-text-muted">Generate the grid, do not hand-write it: a month
    is 35+ cells whose date must agree with its column, and an error there is
    invisible — the grid still looks like a calendar. Both demos above come from
    one function, and a build gate checks every calendar in these docs by
    reading each cell's <code>datetime</code> back against its column heading.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="demo">
    <h2>Three months — repetition, not a setting</h2>
    <Demo code={three} />
    <p class="bo-u-text-muted">Put three <code>.bo-calendar__month</code> tables in one
    <code>.bo-calendar</code>. There is no <code>months</code> option to learn, and they
    wrap to one column when the space runs out, so the same markup serves a wide planning
    screen and a phone.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="demo">
    <h2>Picking a date from it — no JavaScript</h2>
    <Demo code={picker} />
    <p class="bo-u-text-muted">Each day is a real <code>&lt;button type="submit"&gt;</code>
    carrying its own ISO date, inside a plain <code>&lt;form method="get"&gt;</code>. Click
    one and the date is in the URL. <strong>The server decides what is selectable</strong>
    — weekends and the public holiday are rendered <code>disabled</code>, so the rule that
    blocks them lives where the works calendar already lives, not in a client-side config
    you have to keep in sync.</p>
    <p class="bo-u-text-muted">No roving <code>tabindex</code>, no <code>aria-*</code> from
    us, no widget: real buttons in a real table already give keyboard operation, disabled
    semantics and a screen-reader announcement of the column and row. This works with
    scripting switched off.</p>
    <p class="bo-u-text-muted">
      <strong>The honest cost: it is a tab stop per selectable day</strong> — twenty in
      the month above, once the weekends and the holiday are out. A native
      <code>&lt;input type="date"&gt;</code> is one. So use the native input for a plain
      date, and reach for this only when <em>seeing which days are special</em> is part of
      the decision — a delivery date around a plant shutdown, a period-end close. That is
      a narrow case, and it is the whole case.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="demo">
    <h2>Why the framework does not ship a date-picker widget</h2>
    <p>The native date input is good and this framework keeps recommending it. What it
    cannot do is the whole reason for this component: <strong>its only constraints are
    <code>min</code>, <code>max</code> and <code>step</code></strong> — which describe a
    <em>range</em>. A works calendar is a <em>set</em>: eight public holidays, a two-week
    shutdown, the last working day of each period. There is no attribute for that, and no
    API to mark a day.</p>
    <p>So the division of labour is: <strong>the browser owns the ordinary case, this owns
    the case where the marks matter.</strong> Most date entry is ordinary — use the native
    input. When the marks are part of the decision, the section above picks from this grid
    with real submit buttons and no JavaScript.</p>
    <p class="bo-u-text-muted">What is still refused is the <em>widget</em>: a scripted
    popup with roving <code>tabindex</code>, its own ARIA and a month-navigation model to
    re-implement. That is a large surface to own badly, and nothing above needs it —
    which is the test for whether refusing was right.</p>
    <p class="bo-u-text-muted">It is a real <code>&lt;table&gt;</code> because a month is
    tabular data. That buys row and column semantics, <code>scope</code> headers and a
    caption for free: a screen reader announces "Tuesday, 12" without a line of ARIA.
    Each day carries a <code>&lt;time datetime&gt;</code>, so the machine-readable date is
    in the markup rather than inferred from the number.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="demo">
    <h2>Markup</h2>
    <pre><code>{single}</code></pre>
  </section>

  <ClassRef component="calendar" />
  <ApiTable component="calendar" notes={[
    'data-day is written by your app per render, like data-row-state — it is app state, not a variant.',
    'Every marked day needs its meaning in text; the colour and shape are the visible half only.',
    'A day may be a plain span, a link or a button. Only the last two get hover and pointer affordances.',
  ]} />
